// Proofs for the identities identities.hana states, in the strategy
// language of docs/proving.md. An identity with no entry here is proved by
// `diagram` alone — this file holds exactly the claims that need direction.

// The right-hand side is written as the call it is, and the engine is not
// allowed to spend a sentence's definition on its own: say so.
proof identities::testing_a_test_by_name = inline diagram;

// The arm recomputes the very test its branch decided. Nothing stands
// between an arm and what it reads, so the driver identifies the two
// computations by `dedup` alone, and what is left is a branch answering
// with the very value it turns on.
//
// `specialize-bool` is the row that says a bool a branch turned on is
// `true` where the select reads it, and it wants the coercion in its
// window — so the proof spends `codomain` on each side to write the
// promise down as a box, and the branch layer takes both sides home.
// `cases` is not what this needs: a split carves the left side's blocks and proves each
// against the *whole* right side, and this right side tests the same wire,
// so neither case would be true on its own.
proof identities::specializing_a_tested_value =
    both(decide) both(fire(codomain)) diagram;

// The reader-split, said outright. The right side reads one of a shared
// wire's two readings through the cancelling pair and leaves the other on
// the wire, so the unselective statement — every reader through, the
// boundary included — is not this claim. `for(out1)` names the reader
// that follows: the top reading goes through the pair, the deep one keeps
// the wire it always read, and the two sides are the one diagram.
//
// `diagram` alone would also close this, by taking the pair *down* on the
// right instead — which is why the proof is written: it pins the other
// road, the stated introduction landing on one reader of a shared wire,
// as a claim the corpus checks and not only a unit test.
proof identities::a_pair_for_one_reader_only =
    lhs(on(in0, tuple-cancel, for(out1))) diagram;

// The two unpackings of a coercion. Both grow the graph — they say a
// coercion as the program it is — so no list drives them and the proof
// names the one it wants. What follows is the ordinary decision procedure:
// once the branch is there, the branch layer takes it from both sides.
proof identities::as_bool_is_the_branch_it_makes = lhs(fire(as-bool-branch)) diagram;
proof identities::as_int_is_a_guarded_identity = lhs(fire(coercion-guard)) diagram;
proof identities::as_tuple_is_a_guarded_identity = lhs(fire(coercion-guard)) diagram;

// The other splitter, and the one that spends a branch rather than making
// one: the left side answers with a branch, so each of its blocks answers
// for itself against the right side. The `then` block is the coercion and
// wants the unpacking named at it; the `else` block is already the right
// side, so its goal closes before any step runs and the arm is left out.
proof identities::a_branch_whose_blocks_agree =
    select-same (then: lhs(fire(as-bool-branch)));

// The commuting conversion, spent where a proof asks for it. The row grows
// the graph — it duplicates the region the branch grows over — so no list
// drives it and the proof names it; once the branch has moved past the
// `negate`, the two sides are the one diagram.
proof identities::what_follows_a_branch_runs_inside_it =
    lhs(fire(select-hoist)) diagram;

// `fire` would take the first select it is offered and leave the other
// answer of the same branch standing. The aim names the branch instead —
// the wire, and every select on it — so one written step is one firing per
// answer, and what follows is the ordinary decision procedure.
proof identities::a_branch_of_two_answers_grows_over_what_follows_it =
    lhs(at(selects-on(in0), select-hoist)) diagram;

// And the sibling row, at the port that one cannot reach: the branch that
// turns on what a branch answered runs under it. One firing again, and the
// same shape of proof — the row grows the graph, so no list drives it and the
// proof names it.
proof identities::a_branch_in_a_condition_runs_under_it =
    lhs(fire(cond-hoist)) diagram;

// And the drive that spends the same row to a fixpoint. `tree` hoists every
// branch past everything but another branch, one firing at a time, until no
// box that is not a select reads what a select answers — the decision tree,
// which is the shape the right side is written in. Two firings here, and the
// order is the drive's own: the second branch reads what the first one
// answers, so the second moves first and the first follows it.
proof identities::the_branches_end_up_at_the_output = lhs(tree) diagram;

// The lemma. Left-only on purpose: two rows drive `not ; not` onto the branch
// it makes, and the right side is left exactly as written, so what the proof
// records is a run from one side to the other rather than two runs meeting in
// the middle. That is what `by` can carry; a proof that met in the middle
// would have to be inverted first, and nothing does that yet.
proof identities::a_double_negative_is_the_branch_it_makes =
    lhs(fire(not-not)) lhs(fire(as-bool-branch));

// And the claim that spends it. `by` finds the lemma's left side in this
// goal's left side and carries the lemma's own steps in — no new law, no new
// axiom, and the third `not` left exactly where it was, still reading what the
// branch now leaves.
proof identities::three_negatives_are_a_branch_and_a_negative =
    lhs(by identities::a_double_negative_is_the_branch_it_makes);

// Commutativity, named where it is wanted. `comm` permutes rather than
// shrinking — a driver would swap the same two operands forever — so no list
// hands it out, and one firing is the whole proof: the crossing was never a
// box, so what the left side and the right side differ by is the operand
// order, and that is exactly what the row exchanges.
proof identities::the_operands_of_a_sum_are_interchangeable = lhs(fire(comm));

// The substitution, and the one claim in the corpus that needs it. `cases`
// alone would leave the true case as `x + 1` against `8`, with nothing to
// say that `x` is `7` there.
proof identities::a_tested_value_is_what_it_was_tested_against =
    cases-equal(#t) (true: diagram, false: diagram);
